## Example

Fix $T_*>0$ kelvins, an initial temperature $T_{\mathrm{in}}\in(0,T_*]$, a ratio $r\in(0,1)$, and an operation duration $\tau_*>0$ seconds. Define the model's zero-temperature boundary to be $D_0=\{0\}$ and its positive-temperature states to be $D_+=(0,T_*]$. Each admitted operation maps $T$ to $rT$ and takes duration $\tau_*$. An admitted finite protocol is any finite chain of these operations; write $T_N$ for its endpoint after $N$ updates. The example proves that this restricted protocol family cannot reach $D_0$ in finitely many operations; it does not prove that a real refrigerator or every thermodynamic model has this update rule.

## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** The stipulated state sets, initial temperature, operation map, and fixed finite duration in the Example.

[F1] [[def-td-si-quantities]]: Temperature is measured in kelvins and duration in seconds.

[F2] [[def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential]]: $T>0$ denotes positive absolute thermodynamic temperature on the stated chart.

[F3] [[def-natural-numbers]]: A finite chain has a length $N\in\mathbb N$.

[F4] [[thm-induction-principle]]: A property holding at $0$ and preserved from $N$ to $N+1$ holds for every $N\in\mathbb N$.

[F5] [[def-ordered-field]]: Products of positive reals are positive, and multiplying positive inequalities preserves order.

## Verification

1.1 Let $P(N)$ mean that the endpoint after $N$ admitted operations lies in $D_+$. For $N=0$, the endpoint is the stipulated $T_{\mathrm{in}}>0$, so $P(0)$ holds. [given, F3, base]

2.1 Assume $P(N)$ [IH]. The stipulated operation sends its current temperature $T_N\in(0,T_*]$ to $rT_N$; because $0<r<1$, $0<rT_N<T_N\le T_*$, so the next endpoint satisfies $P(N+1)$. This uses the positive-temperature coordinate on the declared chart. [step 1.1, given, F2, F3, F5, algebra]

3.1 By [F4], steps 1.1 and 2.1 imply $P(N)$ for every finite $N\in\mathbb N$. Every admitted finite protocol has such a length by [F3], and its duration is $N\tau_*<\infty$ by the stipulated finite $\tau_*$. Since $D_+\cap D_0=\varnothing$, none reaches zero. This conclusion is only for the stipulated protocol model. [F1, F3, F4, step 1.1, step 2.1, algebra, discharge-induction] ∎
